    "['area [area-id]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'area 0', 'parent_command': ['interface [parameter1]', 'ip ospf [parameter1] area [parameter2]'], 'root': 'interface [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\narea 0\\n##\\n\\nExplanation: \\n- In Cisco IOS, OSPF area numbers are typically represented in decimal (not dotted-decimal notation like Huawei)\\n- \"0.0.0.0\" in Huawei is equivalent to area 0 (backbone area) in Cisco\\n- The command structure remains similar but with simplified area numbering', 'trans_template': 'area 0'}]]": 1,
    "['network [ip-address] [wildcard-mask]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'network 1.1.1.1 0.0.0.0 area 0', 'parent_command': ['interface [parameter1]', 'ip ospf [parameter1] area [parameter2]', 'area 0'], 'root': 'interface [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nnetwork 1.1.1.1 0.0.0.0 area 0\\n##\\n\\nThe Huawei OSPF network command `network 1.1.1.1 0.0.0.0` specifies exactly the 1.1.1.1/32 host route to be advertised in OSPF area 0. In Cisco, the equivalent command requires explicit area specification, and the wildcard mask 0.0.0.0 (exact match in Huawei) corresponds to a /32 prefix in Cisco.', 'trans_template': 'network 1.1.1.1 0.0.0.0 area 0'}]]": 1,
    "['network [ip-address] [wildcard-mask]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'network 10.1.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0', 'parent_command': ['interface [parameter1]', 'ip ospf [parameter1] area [parameter2]', 'area 0'], 'root': 'interface [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nnetwork 10.1.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:  \\nIn Cisco IOS, the OSPF `network` command requires specifying the area directly in the command (unlike Huawei where the area is configured under a separate `area` subcommand). The wildcard mask `0.0.0.255` remains the same in both vendors. The area `0` is represented as `0` in Cisco (equivalent to Huawei's `0.0.0.0`).\", 'trans_template': 'network 10.1.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0'}]]": 1,
